          <div class="hot-take">🔥 Draft hot take: “It works on my machine” is not a deployment strategy, even if the machine is very confident.</div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="avatar" alt="Avatar of Pruthvi" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 160 160'%3E%3Crect width='160' height='160' rx='80' fill='%23C58F68'/%3E%3Ccircle cx='80' cy='80' r='58' fill='%23F4E5D4'/%3E%3Ctext x='80' y='98' text-anchor='middle' font-family='system-ui,sans-serif' font-size='52' font-weight='700' fill='%23744A35'%3EP%3C/text%3E%3C/svg%3E">
          <h3>Pruthvi</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science · Paderborn University · 2026</p>
          <p class="bio">Starting the M.Sc. Computer Science program at Paderborn University. Their own introduction is still in the queue—unlike software, people shouldn’t be generated from defaults.</p>
          <div class="hot-take">🔥 Draft hot take: A code review comment that asks a good question beats an AI that confidently guesses the answer.</div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p class="eyebrow">The team’s shared view</p>
        <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <p class="future-intro">
          AI is already a useful collaborator: it can help explore unfamiliar code, sketch alternatives,
          and turn a blank page into a first draft. It is not a substitute for understanding the problem,
          checking the result, or being accountable when production has feelings about Friday afternoon.
        </p>
        <div class="future-grid">
          <article class="future-card">
            <h3>Today: faster starts, same responsibility</h3>
            <p>
              We expect AI tools to speed up routine work, but generated code still needs tests,
              context, and a human who can explain why it belongs in the project. “The model said so”
              is not a test case.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="future-card">
            <h3>In 5–10 years: engineers orchestrate, verify, and design</h3>
            <p>
              We think engineers will spend more time expressing intent, connecting systems, reviewing
              agent work, and making careful trade-offs. The craft will shift—not vanish—and debugging
              will probably outlive every prediction that debugging is over.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="closing-line">Our forecast: more capable tools, more important judgment, and at least one mysterious flaky test.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. · 2026 · The Merge Conflicts</p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");

      function updateButton(theme) {
        const isDark = theme === "dark";
        button.textContent = isDark ? "☀" : "☾";
        button.setAttribute("aria-label", isDark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
        button.setAttribute("title", isDark ? "Switch to day mode" : "Switch to night mode");
      }

      updateButton(document.documentElement.dataset.theme || "light");

      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const next = document.documentElement.dataset.theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        document.documentElement.dataset.theme = next;
        updateButton(next);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", next);
        } catch (_) {}
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
